WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
Welcome to Grade 4 English, Unit
4: Community and Communication.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.960
Welcome to Unit 4!

3
00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:14.840
This unit is all about community and communication — the
many ways people share news, ideas, and help with one another.

4
00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:21.020
You will read about a library service, a young
reporter, and how a whole town shares information.

5
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:27.260
You will learn strong new words like service, deliver,
knowledge, information, discover, and priority,

6
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:30.860
and you will use them to talk and
write about your own community.

7
00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:38.300
By the end, you will be ready to share a message clearly,
just like Amal does in this unit's story, “The Circular Plan”.

8
00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:44.580
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

9
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:50.440
By the end of this unit, you will be able to talk
about services and communication in your community,

10
00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.600
using new unit 4 words.

11
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.020
you will also describe places and
things using there is and there are.

12
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:03.340
you will also state facts about your town using the
present simple (“mail carriers deliver letters daily”).

13
00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:05.960
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

14
00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:14.600
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: service,
deliver, information, thought, discover, knowledge.

15
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.760
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

16
00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:24.260
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

17
00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:30.380
The reading sequence includes The Library
That Came to Us and Selma the Young Reporter.

18
00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:31.900
Read actively.

19
00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:37.180
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

20
00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:41.160
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

21
00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:45.700
The first language focus is Countable and Uncountable Nouns.

22
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.280
Some nouns we can count one by one
— one book, two books, three books.

23
00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.100
We call these countable nouns.

24
00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:56.920
Other nouns we cannot count as separate
items — like water, rice, or information.

25
00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.200
We call these uncountable nouns.

26
00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:04.140
Knowing the difference helps you choose the
right words, so your sentences make sense.

27
00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:10.720
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

28
00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:17.360
In speaking, you will complete Speaking
1: Describe a Service in Your Community.

29
00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:22.020
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

30
00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:26.160
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

31
00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.440
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

32
00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:31.200
You are ready to begin.

33
00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:37.460
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

34
00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.040
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

35
00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:45.820
Use AI English for hints and feedback, and
bring your questions to the live sessions.
